From: Ahrion Gallegos <ahrionmgallegos@gmail.com>
Subject: net: let a device decline the forward path walk without failing it

dev_fill_forward_path() treats "this device has no callback" and "this
device's callback says it has nothing to add" as opposite outcomes, when
they are the same statement. The first works: the loop never runs, the
function appends a DEV_PATH_ETHERNET entry for the device and returns 0.
The second fails: any negative return becomes "return -1", the whole walk
is discarded, and flow offload silently never engages for that path.

This makes -EOPNOTSUPP mean what the callers that return it mean by it.

It is not a corner case. Only three functions sit behind
ndo_fill_forward_path in the whole tree, and two of them return
-EOPNOTSUPP to say "not this instance":

  * ppp_fill_forward_path() (drivers/net/ppp/ppp_generic.c:1591)
    returns it at :1599 for a multilink bundle, and at :1610 for any
    channel whose ops lack fill_forward_path. Upstream only
    drivers/net/ppp/pppoe.c:1008 sets that member, so every other PPP
    channel type reaches :1610 -- L2TP among them.
  * mac80211's ieee80211_netdev_fill_forward_path() returns it when the
    driver has no net_fill_forward_path op, which is most of them.

The third, pppoe_fill_forward_path() (pppoe.c:983), returns a bare -1 on
its own error path and is untouched by this.

Neither of the two writes to *path before returning, so there is nothing
partial to clean up -- only the stack slot, which dev_fwd_path() takes
with "int k = stack->num_paths++" before the callback runs. Hence the
decrement.

Three details that are load-bearing rather than stylistic:

  * ret must be cleared. Every caller reads a negative return as failure:
    nft_flow_offload.c:206 gates on ">= 0", and mtk_ppe_offload.c:105-108
    does "if (err) return err". Leaving ret at -EOPNOTSUPP would
    reproduce the exact bug this fixes, one layer up.
  * break, not continue. The callbacks that return -EOPNOTSUPP leave ctx
    untouched, so continuing would hit the WARN_ON_ONCE(last_dev ==
    ctx.dev) guard immediately below.
  * Falling out of the loop is the whole point: ctx.dev is still the
    declining device, so the tail of the function gives it the same
    DEV_PATH_ETHERNET entry a device with no callback would have got.

What it buys on this board: a bridged Wi-Fi client can reach
FLOW_OFFLOAD_XMIT_DIRECT. Today it cannot, because mac80211 returns
-EOPNOTSUPP, the walk is discarded, and nft_dev_path_info() never sees a
path to resolve. With the walk completing, info->indev becomes the AP
netdev -- ARPHRD_ETHER with a real address -- so nft_is_valid_ether_device()
passes and the flow takes the direct xmit path instead of a neighbour
lookup. That is the Wi-Fi half of W0038, which has no other route to it.

Evidence: E2 for the source reading above, at v6.12.103. E1 for the
effect, demonstrated at T0054 by forcing the equivalent state by hand;
see x3000/docs/xdp-methods-tested.md section 23.18 for why a bridged
Wi-Fi client never reaches XMIT_DIRECT without this, and section 24 for
the survey that found the PPP half.

One corroboration worth stating, because it shows the bug bites people
who are not me. MediaTek's own SDK carries
893-tnl-07-l2tp-add-fill-forward-path.patch, which implements
fill_forward_path for L2TP channels. That is a downstream workaround for
ppp_generic.c:1610 -- the vendor hit this and papered over it for the one
channel type they cared about rather than fixing the walk. The hook they
implement is already upstream (include/linux/ppp_channel.h:33); only the
L2TP implementation is theirs.

Note on which mac80211 this describes: OpenWrt builds mac80211 from
backports (package/kernel/mac80211, backports 7.2), not from the in-tree
net/mac80211. The return is at iface.c:939 in pristine v6.12.103 and at
:1001 in the backports copy this image ships. The behaviour is identical;
only the line differs.

Not board-specific, and not MediaTek-specific: any bridged Wi-Fi client
on any driver without a net_fill_forward_path op hits it, as does any
non-PPPoE PPP channel and any multilink bundle. This is W0040 and it is
worth sending upstream on its own merits.

Touches core net only. It does share net/core/dev.c with 871, which an
earlier version of this message wrongly denied, but the two edits are
about 6000 lines apart -- 871 works on dev_set_threaded() and
netif_napi_add_weight(), this one on dev_fill_forward_path() -- so they
do not overlap and apply order between them does not matter. Verified by
applying the full series to v6.12.103 with OpenWrt's generic patches in
place: this one applies at --fuzz=0 and at zero offset after 871. No file
at all is shared with 890, 891, 880 or 892.

Out-of-tree patch for the GL-X3000 lean build.

--- a/net/core/dev.c
+++ b/net/core/dev.c
@@ -746,6 +746,29 @@ int dev_fill_forward_path(const struct n
 
 		memset(path, 0, sizeof(struct net_device_path));
 		ret = ctx.dev->netdev_ops->ndo_fill_forward_path(&ctx, path);
+		if (ret == -EOPNOTSUPP) {
+			/* The device implements the callback but has nothing
+			 * special to say about this particular instance. That
+			 * is the same statement as having no callback at all,
+			 * which falls through below to DEV_PATH_ETHERNET and
+			 * works -- so stop walking and let it.
+			 *
+			 * Two of the three implementations in tree depend on
+			 * this: ppp_fill_forward_path() returns -EOPNOTSUPP
+			 * for a multilink bundle and for any channel type
+			 * whose ops lack fill_forward_path -- every type but
+			 * PPPoE -- and mac80211 returns it when the driver has
+			 * no net_fill_forward_path op. Neither writes to *path
+			 * before returning, so nothing partial is left behind.
+			 *
+			 * dev_fwd_path() has already taken a slot, so give it
+			 * back, and clear ret because every caller reads a
+			 * negative return as failure.
+			 */
+			stack->num_paths--;
+			ret = 0;
+			break;
+		}
 		if (ret < 0)
 			return -1;
 
